> >  D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
>
> One of the key problems here, I think, is that the enemies
> that are *supposed* to present a major challenge - to wit,
> the demon lords and princes ... don't.

I think I agree.

> It would be a good thing, then, to determine how those
> battles could be improved upon.

For one thing, I don't think teleporting to the up stairs
to heal every 0.5 turns makes a monster dangerous.  Hard
to kill, yes, and annoying, but not dangerous.  A monster
that stands its ground and fights would be scarier to me,
(if it's got the stats to be a credible threat), even,
and perhaps even especially, on a non-teleport level.
This is why I proposed named unique high-level player-
like characters.

> This being (a new version of) Hell, what would the greatest asset
> of a demon prince / lord be? In my mind, it should be their
> ability to command lesser demons and send them on the attack.
> Since even an experienced player fears a summon storm, perhaps
> one possibility is to have demon princes / lords gate in minions
> more frequently.

I can sort of see that, but only if the minions they gate in are
potentially an actual threat to a post-castle player character.
If Asmodeus starts gating in a whole bunch of imps and manes,
that would just be incredibly stupid...

> Of course, that isn't necessarily very interesting if the player
> can easily kill those lesser minions. As such, one additional
> possibility that has crossed my mind is to rethink the level of
> difficulty that regular demons should have, and/or possibly
> consider adding more types of demons. (After all, a hostile
> Archon is considered a fairly challenging foe under many
> circumstances; why aren't there any regular demons that could
> provide that?)

I think we should resist the urge to add a whole bunch of new
moster types, SLASH'EM style.  However, I can see adding one
or two demon types, or, better yet, making a couple of the
existing ones more interesting and/or dangerous.

> As an aside, while I was developing GruntHack, I concluded that
> the teleporting behaviour the demon lords and princes (and other
> covetous monsters) exhibit is a bug - one caused by simply reusing
> the code from the Wizard of Yendor wholesale, without consideration
> to the fact that most of the demon princes aren't supposed to be
> able to teleport (as evidenced by their monster definitions).

I'm not convinced that the behavior was unintended (part of the
usual definition of a software bug) and yet lasted for so long
without ever making it onto a bug list.

However, I can certainly see an argument to the effect that it
was a poorly considered feature, the ramifications of which
should have been thought through better.

> I think that addressing this has caused the battles against the
> demon princes to make a little bit more sense and be slightly
> less annoying and slightly more enjoyable, if less difficult;
> whether this is a step in the right direction is debatable, but
> I believe it is.

It's worth thinking about.


